From: "Pluet, LLC" <358065800+pluetllc@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] video: say whether a Dolby Vision RPU has a full enhancement
 layer (video-params/dolby-vision-el)

A Dolby Vision profile 7 stream carries an enhancement layer that is either
minimal (MEL: its residual is a no-op, the base layer and the RPU are the
whole picture) or full (FEL: the picture needs the layer). VideoToolbox
decodes neither: FFmpeg converts profile 7 to profile 8.1 and drops the
layer (FFmpeg 0003/0300). For a MEL stream that loses nothing; for a FEL
stream the player should ask whether to show Dolby Vision without the layer
or the HDR10 base. The demuxer's configuration record cannot tell the two
apart: only the RPU's NLQ parameters do.

mp_image_from_av_frame() now reads them from FFmpeg's AVDOVIMetadata into
mp_image_params.dovi_el, decided as libdovi's RpuDataNlq::is_mel() (what
dovi_tool reports as "Profile: 7 (MEL)" or "(FEL)"): no residual (the
disable_residual_flag, a non-zero rpu_format layout, or no NLQ method) is
none; NLQ offset 0, vdr_in_max 1.0 and a zero linear dead zone in all three
components is MEL; anything else is FEL. The field is dynamic metadata, as
repr.dovi is: mp_image_params_equal() does not compare it, so a stream whose
frames disagree never reconfigures the chain.

video-params/dolby-vision-el (and video-dec-params/..., video-out-params/...)
is "mel" or "fel", unavailable otherwise.

scripts/test_dovi_rpu_layers.sh compiles the decision (the marked "dovi el
kind" region of video/mp_image.c) against a host libavcodec of the
patched FFmpeg and checks it on every RPU in libdovi's test assets against
dovi_tool's own verdict, MEL, FEL and RPUs without a residual alike.

